1
00:00:08,076 --> 00:00:12,810
By a spell, a piece of wood appears
as an elephant. There is no elephant.

2
00:00:12,890 --> 00:00:14,350
But is there really wood?

3
00:00:15,160 --> 00:00:17,788
I am John Tan, known as Thusness.

4
00:00:17,868 --> 00:00:21,746
These are my own notes, from my
own practice and contemplation.

5
00:00:22,556 --> 00:00:27,218
In the Dependent Arising series I say
more than once that I part from Yogācāra,

6
00:00:27,298 --> 00:00:28,825
the school of Mind-Only.

7
00:00:28,905 --> 00:00:31,529
I said where: making mind the floor.

8
00:00:31,609 --> 00:00:34,876
That is fair to Yogācāra
as it is commonly read.

9
00:00:35,688 --> 00:00:39,281
But Yogācāra has a key of
its own, the three natures.

10
00:00:39,361 --> 00:00:43,579
Someone who knows it will ask whether
I have met it. This is where I meet it.

11
00:00:44,380 --> 00:00:46,804
Vasubandhu puts it in three lines.

12
00:00:46,884 --> 00:00:49,060
The dependent nature is what appears.

13
00:00:49,140 --> 00:00:51,804
It arises from causes and conditions.

14
00:00:51,884 --> 00:00:55,622
The imagined nature is how it
appears: as a thing out there,

15
00:00:55,702 --> 00:00:57,348
grasped by a me in here.

16
00:00:57,428 --> 00:01:01,401
And the perfected nature is that
it never exists the way it appears.

17
00:01:02,216 --> 00:01:04,110
His image is a magic show.

18
00:01:04,190 --> 00:01:07,439
By a spell, a piece of wood
appears as an elephant.

19
00:01:07,519 --> 00:01:09,898
The elephant is the imagined nature.

20
00:01:09,978 --> 00:01:12,235
The appearing is the dependent nature.

21
00:01:12,315 --> 00:01:15,691
And that there is no elephant
in it is the perfected nature.

22
00:01:16,500 --> 00:01:18,503
Asaṅga gives another image.

23
00:01:18,583 --> 00:01:23,381
Gold-bearing earth, before firing,
shows the clay and not the gold.

24
00:01:23,461 --> 00:01:27,185
Fired in wisdom, the clay
goes, and the gold shows.

25
00:01:27,265 --> 00:01:29,278
The dependent nature is the earth.

26
00:01:29,358 --> 00:01:31,574
Seen wrongly, it is defiled.

27
00:01:31,654 --> 00:01:33,616
Seen rightly, it is pure.

28
00:01:34,432 --> 00:01:38,333
A sūtra of this school takes the old
saying that all things lack a nature of

29
00:01:38,413 --> 00:01:40,742
their own. It does not deny it.

30
00:01:40,822 --> 00:01:43,899
It explains it, one lack for each nature.

31
00:01:44,708 --> 00:01:47,413
The imagined lacks a
defining mark of its own,

32
00:01:47,493 --> 00:01:49,048
like a flower in the sky.

33
00:01:49,128 --> 00:01:53,505
The dependent lacks a nature of its
own in its arising, like a magic show.

34
00:01:53,585 --> 00:01:56,716
And the perfected is the
absence of a self itself.

35
00:01:57,528 --> 00:02:00,274
And it warns: take the
saying as nothing at all,

36
00:02:00,354 --> 00:02:02,086
and you fall into nihilism.

37
00:02:02,884 --> 00:02:04,575
Much of this I can walk with.

38
00:02:04,655 --> 00:02:06,939
What appears arises in dependence.

39
00:02:07,019 --> 00:02:10,662
It is vivid, it works, and
it is not what it seems.

40
00:02:10,742 --> 00:02:12,523
The elephant is never there.

41
00:02:12,603 --> 00:02:14,765
And seeing that is not losing the wood.

42
00:02:14,845 --> 00:02:18,397
That is close to what I mean
by empty, and not ghostly.

43
00:02:19,208 --> 00:02:21,157
But I part at three places.

44
00:02:21,237 --> 00:02:23,838
And they are narrower
than mind is the floor.

45
00:02:24,636 --> 00:02:28,297
The first. In the Tibetan
presentation of Mind-Only,

46
00:02:28,377 --> 00:02:31,544
the dependent nature must
exist by its own character.

47
00:02:31,624 --> 00:02:34,384
Something real has to
be there, it is said,

48
00:02:34,464 --> 00:02:36,259
for the imagining to rest on.

49
00:02:36,339 --> 00:02:38,260
Otherwise, nihilism.

50
00:02:39,064 --> 00:02:42,480
Here I side with the Prāsaṅgika
reading of Nāgārjuna.

51
00:02:42,560 --> 00:02:45,388
No own character, not even conventionally.

52
00:02:45,468 --> 00:02:49,151
Things work because they are empty, not
because something real lies under the

53
00:02:49,231 --> 00:02:50,431
imagining.

54
00:02:51,068 --> 00:02:56,468
The second. Yogācāra explains karma across
time with a storehouse consciousness,

55
00:02:56,548 --> 00:02:58,728
which carries seeds from life to life.

56
00:02:59,544 --> 00:03:02,675
I agree with Tsongkhapa in
refusing the storehouse.

57
00:03:02,755 --> 00:03:07,784
I part from him by not naming any linking
factor at all, even a designated one.

58
00:03:07,864 --> 00:03:09,957
Neither carried, nor cut.

59
00:03:10,037 --> 00:03:14,210
As Candrakīrti puts it, an
action does not inherently cease,

60
00:03:14,290 --> 00:03:16,049
so nothing needs to carry it.

61
00:03:16,852 --> 00:03:21,668
The third. Yogācāra holds that each
moment of awareness knows itself.

62
00:03:21,748 --> 00:03:25,082
Otherwise, it asks, how
could I remember seeing?

63
00:03:25,880 --> 00:03:30,696
Candrakīrti answers that memory arises
in dependence on the earlier seeing.

64
00:03:30,776 --> 00:03:32,720
No extra knower is needed.

65
00:03:32,800 --> 00:03:35,505
Clarity is never absent from what appears.

66
00:03:35,585 --> 00:03:38,902
But made into something that knows
itself apart from what appears,

67
00:03:38,982 --> 00:03:40,809
it is the knower coming back.

68
00:03:41,604 --> 00:03:43,281
These three come to one thing.

69
00:03:43,361 --> 00:03:45,509
Each keeps something real underneath.

70
00:03:45,589 --> 00:03:50,115
A real basis. A real
carrier. A real self-knowing.

71
00:03:50,195 --> 00:03:53,200
Take them away, and what
is left is not less.

72
00:03:53,280 --> 00:03:54,916
The bell still rings.

73
00:03:55,720 --> 00:03:59,381
The commonly read Yogācāra
does keep mind as the floor.

74
00:03:59,461 --> 00:04:03,199
Vasubandhu used the hungry
ghosts and the river of pus.

75
00:04:03,279 --> 00:04:05,644
Shared karma ripening
in the mind is enough,

76
00:04:05,724 --> 00:04:07,891
he said, with no outer river.

77
00:04:08,684 --> 00:04:11,353
Candrakīrti turned the story around.

78
00:04:11,433 --> 00:04:14,288
Mind and object stand or fall together.

79
00:04:14,368 --> 00:04:16,095
The dream proves nothing.

80
00:04:16,175 --> 00:04:19,002
If waking memory shows the
dreaming mind was there,

81
00:04:19,082 --> 00:04:21,394
it shows the dream objects were too.

82
00:04:21,474 --> 00:04:25,026
In the absence of an object,
there is also no intellect.

83
00:04:25,824 --> 00:04:27,610
His answer is my position.

84
00:04:27,690 --> 00:04:31,092
Idealism is materialism turned inside out.

85
00:04:31,900 --> 00:04:35,448
Some scholars read Yogācāra
not as only mind exists,

86
00:04:35,528 --> 00:04:38,074
but as an account of
how experience is built.

87
00:04:38,154 --> 00:04:42,205
On that reading the gap is smaller, and
the three places I named are where it

88
00:04:42,285 --> 00:04:43,485
remains.

89
00:04:44,144 --> 00:04:48,688
There are two keys here. The sūtra
calls its own key the third turning,

90
00:04:48,768 --> 00:04:53,416
the definitive one. It calls the plain
saying, that all things lack a nature,

91
00:04:53,496 --> 00:04:56,845
provisional. Madhyamaka says the reverse.

92
00:04:57,660 --> 00:05:01,239
I take Nāgārjuna's reading
as final, for one reason.

93
00:05:01,319 --> 00:05:04,962
It leaves nothing standing
underneath, and it does not need to.

94
00:05:05,042 --> 00:05:07,095
What appears is dependent all the way.

95
00:05:07,175 --> 00:05:09,491
And that is exactly why it works.

96
00:05:10,288 --> 00:05:12,522
Śāntarakṣita joined the two.

97
00:05:12,602 --> 00:05:17,468
Yogācāra for everyday seeing, and
the test of one or many for analysis.

98
00:05:17,548 --> 00:05:19,071
I borrow his test.

99
00:05:19,151 --> 00:05:21,643
I do not borrow the everyday mind-only.

100
00:05:22,436 --> 00:05:26,613
This is Yogācāra: the three
natures, and where I part.

101
00:05:27,432 --> 00:05:29,272
The elephant was never there.

102
00:05:29,352 --> 00:05:31,994
And nothing real was
needed under the wood.

